---
import Lecture from '../../../layouts/Lecture.astro';
import CodePlayground from '../../../components/CodePlayground.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/Callout.astro';
import Quiz from '../../../components/Quiz.astro';
---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="03-loops"
  lessonSlug="03-find-max"
  hook="The tracking-variable pattern. Initialize with the FIRST element — not zero — and your function survives the all-negatives case."
>
  <h2>The pattern</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def find_max(numbers):
    best = numbers[0]              # initialize with the FIRST element
    for x in numbers[1:]:          # loop over the REST
        if x > best:               # update when we see something bigger
            best = x
    return best

print(find_max([3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6]))   # → 9
print(find_max([-7, -1, -100]))             # → -1
print(find_max([42]))                       # → 42`}
  />

  <h2>The trap: initializing to 0</h2>
  <CodePlayground
    caption="What does this return for all-negative input?"
    code={`def find_max_BUGGY(numbers):
    best = 0
    for x in numbers:
        if x > best:
            best = x
    return best

print(find_max_BUGGY([-7, -1, -100]))   # → 0   ← not even in the input!`}
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="Why this happens">
    <p>
      Every element is less than <code>0</code>, so the <code>if x &gt; best</code>
      branch never fires. The function returns <code>0</code> — which isn't
      in the input list at all. The autograder's <code>test_all_negatives</code>
      catches this.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <Quiz
    question="What's the SAFEST initial value if the input could be ANY positive or negative numbers (but is non-empty)?"
    choices={[
      { text: "0",  explain: "Fails for all-negative input." },
      { text: "numbers[0]", correct: true, explain: "Right. Initialize with a real element from the input, then loop over the rest." },
      { text: "min(numbers)", explain: "Would work, but you'd be using a built-in to write find_max — which defeats the lesson." },
      { text: "1_000_000", explain: "Just as bad as 0 — fails for inputs whose max is larger than 1_000_000." },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>The pattern generalizes</h2>
  <p>
    The same three-step shape — initialize / update conditionally / return —
    drives <code>find_min</code>, <code>find_longest_word</code>,
    <code>count_evens</code>, and a dozen others. Once you can write
    <code>find_max</code>, you can write all of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Now you try</h2>
  <p>
    Open <code>03-find-max.py</code>. 6 tests including the all-negatives
    edge case and a check that you didn't use the built-in <code>max()</code>.
  </p>
</Lecture>
